// Ladle); the card "The next day"; Himeji town (#36, Supper For A Voice); the foot of Mount Shosha and the women's hall
// (#37, What A Ship Gives Its Lady: the lock close-up and the hall); the small door at sunset and every line of the Son
// (#38, A Small Door At Sunset); "Mind The Fire"; the step's new line; the map's far lines for five legs. WRITE-21A writes
// the other half (edo-c.en.js); the phase-B lead joins the halves.
//   "line.id": ["who", "What they say."]
// Scene-shorts: walk (the card), akashi, walk2 (the card), town, shosha, lock (the close-up), hall, night (the cut-scene),
// map (the far lines). A look is edo.<short>.<thing>.<who>, a use edo.<short>.<thing>.use.<who>, a use with a carried thing
// edo.<short>.<thing>.with.<item>.<who>; a look that changes with the story has a state word before the who
// (edo.shosha.doors.open.mom). The talks are trees (js/content/talk/edo-d.js); an option is a line here, said by the one
// who picks it. S/work-21-writeB/ids.md says when every line is said, and which hooks and facts the trees use.
//
// THE RULES OF THE ACT, as these lines keep them:
//   - Nobody in the family lies. Every option the player can pick for them is true; the comedy is in exactly true
//     answers ("I'm only SEVEN." "Girls are women, for this."; Ryōkai's "I heard nothing I understood.").
//   - Scripture inside quotation marks is the King James Version exactly: Psalm 61:1-2, Mom's own prayer at the foot of
//     the mountain she may not climb (edo.shosha.psalm.1 to .3, three boxes: together the two verses letter for letter),
//     and Isaiah 49:15, Mom to her son as the small door closes (edo.hall.sunset.1 to .3: "sucking child", lower-case
//     "yea"). Big Sister says the references aloud, as only she may ("Psalm 61.", "Isaiah 49:15.").
//   - Japanese words, few, each glossed once by its speaker or by Big Sister: "tako" (Heita; Big Sister's gloss: it is
//     one of her forty food words), "hi no yōjin" (Tōbei glosses it himself: "Mind the fire."),
//     and round twenty's "arigatō" (Little Sister, to everyone) and "Kannon-sama" (O-Ume). "mon" is the coin's name, as
//     the deck taught it. Nobody remarks on the language; the voice in the hall is a language nobody there knows, and the
//     lines say so only as the story needs ("I don't know the words. She sounds kind.").
//   - Big Sister's facts here are the fact files' (S/work-21-factsB, -factsC, -factsD and round twenty's two): Akashi's
//     castle with four turrets and no keep; the strait two and a half miles across with a tide like a river; green pines
//     and white sand; octopus pots on lines; the cotton office and the domain selling straight to Edo; paper money; the
//     spare month; women barred from the mountain since the fourteen hundreds; the east path about an hour's climb; name
//     slips and how they are written; the moon rabbit on the waves, a woman's pattern; locks pushed shut with no key;
//     trick locks with several keys, a hidden keyhole and a sound when solved; Kannon's name; the Funadama's list; the
//     Kansai rice ball and the alms of one rice ball and one slice of pickle; Basho's ten hiki at Suma. ready.md lists
//     each with its item. Nothing else goes in as a fact.
//   - Nothing frightening: the voice is a hungry boy; the octopus lets go of the ladle for a crab, the crab gets away,
//     and Heita has his supper; the night man is kind; O-Ume's son, lost at sea thirty years ago, is said once, plainly, by her.
//   - The box (WRITE-25: rewritten to docs/WRITING.md in round twenty-five, which wins over these rules): at most 115
//     characters spoken and 150 the narrator's (Scripture aside), one idea, the punch last; contractions for the family;
//     at most one word in capitals a box; few exclamation marks.

